Use gmtime_r, because gmtime shares one static buffer between all threads.

gmtime returns a pointer into a single buffer that belongs to the C library,
so two threads that call it at the same time overwrite each other's result.
Nix calls it at two places, and both format a `lastModified` timestamp:

  * `describe` (src/libflake/lockfile.cc), which `LockFile::diff` calls, and
    `lockFlake` calls that.
  * `emitTreeAttrs` (src/libexpr/primops/fetchTree.cc), which `callFlake`
    calls, and which the `fetchTree`/`fetchGit` primops also reach during an
    ordinary evaluation.

`nix` the command evaluates on one thread and never meets this. nanopynix
gives each evaluator its own thread, so two evaluators that touch a flake at
the same time do. ThreadSanitizer reports it:

    WARNING: ThreadSanitizer: data race
      Write of size 8 by thread T28:
        #0 gmtime
        #1 describe ../lockfile.cc:353
        #15 nix::flake::lockFlake(...) ../flake.cc:804
      Previous write of size 8 by thread T25:
        #0 gmtime
        #1 nix::emitTreeAttrs(...) ../primops/fetchTree.cc:167
        #2 nix::flake::callFlake(...) ../flake.cc:951
      Location is global '_tmbuf' of size 56 (libc.so.6+0x2013e0)
    SUMMARY: ThreadSanitizer: data race ../lockfile.cc:353 in describe

gmtime_r writes a buffer that the caller owns, and it returns that same
pointer, so each call site stays one expression. `<ctime>` is included at
both places already.

This is nanopynix issue #90. The defect belongs to Nix, and this patch is the
local repair until an upstream release carries one.

This file applies to 2.36 and to git. `nix-2.34-gmtime-not-thread-safe.patch`
is the same change for 2.34 and for 2.35. Only the call site of `attrs.alloc`
in fetchTree.cc differs: 2.36 gives that function a second argument, the
position of the call.

diff -ruN a/src/libexpr/primops/fetchTree.cc b/src/libexpr/primops/fetchTree.cc
--- a/src/libexpr/primops/fetchTree.cc	2026-09-02 10:15:37.988231445 +0200
+++ b/src/libexpr/primops/fetchTree.cc	2026-09-02 10:15:46.695591603 +0200
@@ -167,8 +167,11 @@
 
     if (auto lastModified = input.getLastModified()) {
         attrs.alloc("lastModified", callPos).mkInt(*lastModified);
+        // See the comment in libflake/lockfile.cc's describe(): gmtime shares
+        // one static buffer between every thread, and gmtime_r does not.
+        struct tm tmBuf;
         attrs.alloc("lastModifiedDate", callPos)
-            .mkString(fmt("%s", std::put_time(std::gmtime(&*lastModified), "%Y%m%d%H%M%S")), state.mem);
+            .mkString(fmt("%s", std::put_time(::gmtime_r(&*lastModified, &tmBuf), "%Y%m%d%H%M%S")), state.mem);
     }
 
     v.mkAttrs(attrs);
diff -ruN a/src/libflake/lockfile.cc b/src/libflake/lockfile.cc
--- a/src/libflake/lockfile.cc	2026-09-02 10:15:37.996489564 +0200
+++ b/src/libflake/lockfile.cc	2026-09-02 10:15:38.347194627 +0200
@@ -349,8 +349,14 @@
 {
     auto s = fmt("'%s'", flakeRef.to_string());
 
-    if (auto lastModified = flakeRef.input.getLastModified())
-        s += fmt(" (%s)", std::put_time(std::gmtime(&*lastModified), "%Y-%m-%d"));
+    if (auto lastModified = flakeRef.input.getLastModified()) {
+        // gmtime writes one static buffer that every thread shares, so two
+        // evaluator threads formatting a lastModified corrupt each other's
+        // result. gmtime_r writes the caller's buffer and returns it, which
+        // keeps this a single expression.
+        struct tm tmBuf;
+        s += fmt(" (%s)", std::put_time(::gmtime_r(&*lastModified, &tmBuf), "%Y-%m-%d"));
+    }
 
     return s;
 }
